  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1232 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nMidnight infrared satellite imagery depicted a clear sky over \nSW KS, and surface observations outlined a continued\nunseasonably warm boundary layer, with temperatures holding way\nabove normal, mainly in the 70s. A strong low level jet was in\nplace, with 850 mb winds near 60 mph keeping south winds\nelevated at the ground. South winds will remain elevated through\nsunrise, with temperatures struggling to fall below 70 at many \nlocations. \n\nPersistence will be a good forecasting tool into Monday, with\nthe synoptic pattern observed over the weekend not changing. A\nstrong trough over the Great Basin will maintain SWly midlevel\nflow over SW KS another day, with elevated to strong south to\nsouthwest winds, and unseasonably hot afternoon temperatures.\nTemperatures of 90-93 are expected to be common at 3 pm, about\n20 degrees above normal for mid October. SW winds from the NBM\nwere several knots too weak, and increased them with 20-30 mph\nwinds prevailing this afternoon.\n\nThe strong closed midlevel cyclone over the Great Basin is\nforecast to reach the Grand Canyon by 7 pm Tuesday, as strong\nhigh pressure remains anchored over the lower Mississippi\nvalley. SWly midlevel flow will increase to over 50 kts as a\nresult. Associated lee cyclogenesis is expected over southeast \nColorado, with a cold front bisecting SW KS by 7 pm Tuesday. A \nboundary intersection between this cold front and the dryline is\nexpected somewhere in SW KS, with NAM placing the best \ninstability/shear parameter space for supercell thunderstorms \nsoutheast of this boundary intersection, from Dodge City \nsoutheastward Tuesday evening. Many CAMs and the global models\nall suggest thunderstorm development in these southeast zones\nTuesday evening, with NBM pops in the likely/definite category\nquite reasonable. With an environment supportive of rotating\nsupercells, some storms are expected to be severe, with hail \n1-2 inches in diameter, and wind gusts of 60-70 mph. If a\ndiscrete storm mode can be maintained - ahead of the cold front\nremaining surface-based - some tornado potential is evident. \nSevere weather risk is expected to be highest southeast of a \nLiberal-Dodge City-Larned line 3-10 pm Tuesday. More lower 90s \nare expected ahead of the advancing cold front Tuesday \nafternoon. \n\nIncreasing indications that a dramatically colder air mass will\narrive rather aggressively Wednesday, with stratus, northeast\nwinds, and afternoon temperatures restricted to the 50s the most\nlikely outcome. NBM is still way too warm, but it is trending\ncooler with each update. The coolest solutions will likely\nverify, with NAM showing stratus and drizzle persisting all day\nwith its classic light drizzle QPF signature. With surface cold\nair aggressively moving southward, this is expected to push any\nsurface-based severe threat south of SW KS, but overrunning,\nstratiform showers and embedded thunder are expected north of\nthe frontal boundary Wednesday afternoon/night. \n\nNo later than Thursday morning, much cooler air will be rushing\ninto SW KS on strong north winds. NBM/MEX guidance continue to\ntrend cooler with each successive run, with afternoon\ntemperatures restricted to the 60s. Friday appears to be the\ncoolest day, with afternoon temperatures struggling to escape\nthe 50s even in full sunshine. Strong 1035 mb surface ridging\nwill wedge its way into SW KS, delivering the coolest morning so\nfar this fall season Saturday morning. Light winds will support\nnear ideal radiational cooling sunrise Saturday, with\ntemperatures in the 30s common. Frost/freeze headlines will\nlikely be required Saturday morning.",
